README-CIO -- the starred programs, and the five modules that serve them=======================================================================Some of the names in DOC/INDEX carry a star.  A star means the program needsone of Microware's runtime trap modules: `cio` for nearly all of them, andfor some `math`, `math881`, `csl` or `csl020`.THEY RUN HERE.  All five modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included withMicroware's permission -- SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  You need donothing, supply nothing, and fetch nothing.  The star tells you what aprogram depends on, not what it lacks.This file explains what the star means, and what to do in the two caseswhere you might want a different arrangement.Why so many programs need it----------------------------`cc -qixm` -- the C library in a shared trap handler rather than linked intoevery binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and it is why a starredprogram is often a fraction of the size of a trap-free one.  Much of thiscollection was rebuilt trap-free (`-qm`) so it would run with nothing loadedat all; most of the programs under CMDS need no module whatever.  The restwere left as their authors built them.A program that cannot find its trap handler stops at once with    **** Can't install trap handler ****That is the message to recognise.  It is not a broken program, and on thisdisk you should never see it -- if you do, the five modules have been removedor the execution directory is not this disk's CMDS.The list was MEASURED, by running every program on a disk with the fivemodules taken away and matching that message.  It was never derived fromlooking for the string `cio` inside a binary: that proxy is wrong in bothdirections, and this collection has the scars to prove it -- `vi_nocio`contains the string and does not need the module, while `cyberwar`, `gnuchess`and `g` lack it and do.If you would rather use your own--------------------------------You run OS-9, so you have these modules already, and you may prefer your copy-- a different edition, or one matched to your processor.  Two ways:1. KEEP YOUR OWN /dd.  Point os9exec at your system disk and mount this one   alongside, so /dd stays yours and the collection is simply another drive:       OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9disk  OS9H1=/path/to/freeware/dd  os9exec shell       chx /h1/CMDS   Your `cio` is found first, from your own /dd/CMDS.2. REPLACE THE COPIES HERE.  Overwrite them in this disk's CMDS:       copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio       copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math   The disk you end up with then contains modules from your own SDK, so keep   that copy for your own use; the collection as published stays as it is.3. LOAD IT, wherever it is.  A trap handler has to be reachable through   the EXECUTION directory, which is why the message appears at all when   you run from somewhere else.  Putting the module in the module   directory first settles it wherever you are:       load /dd/CMDS/cio   CMDS/load is on this disk (source in SRC/load), so this needs nothing   fetched.  It is also how the six RTF Fortran programs get `os9lib' --   DOC/README-FORTRAN.   MEASURED 2026-08-28, both ways round: with the execution directory   moved to a directory holding no cio, `banner HI' stops with the   message above; after `load /dd/CMDS/cio' in the same session it   draws its banner.`math` is needed only by the programs that do floating point -- seeDOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT, which also explains when you want `math881`instead.DOC/README-RUNNING covers where the collection should be mounted, and thehandful of programs that look for files under /h0.If you take the modules away----------------------------Someone redistributing a subset, or testing what is genuinely free-standing,may want the disk without them.  Remove all five from CMDS and most of theprograms still run -- that is the whole point of the rebuild work.  Thestarred ones stop with the message above until a licensed `cio` is put back.Where a starred program mattered, there is usually a trap-free alternativealready here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, sedt, sed    *vi_cio           vi (the EFFO build)    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat.cio          cat    *rcs and friends  no free replacement on this diskDOC/INDEX marks every one of them, so you can tell before you try.A FEW cio PROGRAMS FAIL EVEN WITH cio PRESENT -- and here is how to tell------------------------------------------------------------------------Two programs on this disk answer with hundreds of lines of    No more memory !!!and do their job not at all:    echo 255 | cvtbase d h    setenv PORT /term ; logisim /dd/DOC/logisim/flipflop.lsiThat message comes from the EMULATOR, not from the program, which is why itreads like a broken machine.  It is neither.  Root cause found 2026-08-31:  A cio program reaches the C library through TRAP #13 with a selector.  The  archives here were linked against a `cio.l' whose table has `_flshbuf' at  selector $41.  Every `cio' MODULE anyone has -- ours, the SDK's -- puts a  MEMORY routine there instead.  So the program's `putc' calls what it thinks  is "flush this FILE" and reaches "allocate this many bytes", handing over  the FILE's ADDRESS as the byte count.  The allocation succeeds, the FILE is  never updated, and the next character does it again.  A few hundred  thousand times.  The cio module that would match those binaries is on none of these disks.WHAT THIS MEANS WHEN YOU MEET IT.  The program is not repairable from hereand no amount of memory helps.  Use the alternative in the table above.WHICH PROGRAMS.  353 modules here link cio; 41 contain the call that can dothis; and all 41 have now been run.  ELEVEN are broken, and they fail in twoquite different ways:  LOUDLY -- hundreds of `No more memory !!!' and no work done:      cvtbase   logisim   unstr  SILENTLY -- the program opens your file, reads NOT ONE BYTE of it, and  reports on it anyway:      cdiff   pagekwic   pagefraz   nroff   etags   cookhash      yacc   xrf  `loan' probably belongs there too.SEEN IN ONE RUN.  `xrf' is the clearest case, because it reads one filecorrectly and never reads the other in the same process.  Given its languagetable it does this:    I$Open  "cdiff.c"     <- the source, 30K    I$Open  "C.XRF"       <- the language table    I$Read  the table                          and it prints "Language table                                               loaded"    ...and cdiff.c is NEVER READ.  Not one byte.The table goes through `read()' and arrives intact.  The source goes throughthe `getc' macro and never arrives at all.  Same program, same run, two files.THE SILENT ONES ARE THE DANGEROUS ONES.  `cdiff' says "MAXLINECOUNT exceeded"on a three-line file; `pagekwic' says "word too long in line 0" on eighteenbytes whose longest word is five; `nroff' and `etags' print nothing at all;`cookhash' prints "000000".  Each looks like a program with a small bug ratherthan one that never read its input.  Worse, the same fault hands the Clibrary's allocator the FILE structure itself as a block to free, so the heapis corrupted as well -- the loud failure at least stops the program.`unstr' needs its real index to show it: `unstr /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes'storms where a three-line test file does not.AND BEWARE THE MESSAGE ITSELF.  A program can print `No more memory !!!' onceor twice for the ordinary reason: it asked for memory and there was none.`sedt' does, and it is not broken by this defect.  The message alone tells younothing; what tells you is hundreds of lines, or a file opened and neverread.  The difference matters: the call onlyfires if the program runs a `putc' or `getc' MACRO on a FILE.  Anything thatprints through `printf', `fprintf' or `fwrite' is unaffected -- which is whyevery one of the 41 prints its own usage text perfectly, and why 368 starredprograms each passed 272,000 bytes of input through with no trouble at all.THE 41 ARE NOT A GUESS.  Two versions of Microware's `cio.l' exist, and abinary shows which one it linked: the highest trap-13 selector in its own stubtable.  Of the 353 cio programs here, 96 carry a table reaching $45, 116 reach$44, and 139 are partial.  EVERY ONE of the 41 is from the $44 group and NOTONE program from the $45 group is affected.  The modules we have dispatch$00..$45, so the $45 library is the matching one.  In it, `putc' and `getc'are ordinary function calls handled inside the module; in the $44 library theyare macros whose slow path calls the broken selectors.  Nothing has to be runto know which group a program is in.The 41, most call sites first.  Two are confirmed to reach them; the restcarry the call and may never run it, so this is a list to be careful withrather than a list of broken programs:    CMDS/yacc               CMDS/xrf                CMDS/etags              CMDS/hexed    CMDS/cvtbase            CMDS/liborder           CMDS/unpacklib          CMDS/sedt    CMDS/GAMES/valspeak     CMDS/diff               CMDS/loan               CMDS/logisim    CMDS/makelex            CMDS/REBUILT/kermit_cio CMDS/GAMES/newsgen      CMDS/chksum    CMDS/crypto             CMDS/dam                CMDS/snap               CMDS/spiff    CMDS/GAMES/blackjak     CMDS/GAMES/poker        CMDS/GAMES/stone        CMDS/ape    CMDS/ascii              CMDS/nroff              CMDS/printf             CMDS/undel    CMDS/unifdef            CMDS/unstr              CMDS/wish               CMDS/GAMES/tess    CMDS/cdiff              CMDS/cookhash           CMDS/lp                 CMDS/pagefraz    CMDS/pagekwic           CMDS/setfont            CMDS/strfile            CMDS/vis    CMDS/GAMES/rpoemIf you meet it in a program not named here, that is the shape to recognise:usage text fine, real work impossible, and the emulator's voice in place ofthe program's.tools/cio_macro_scan.py regenerates this list, and check_disk fails if thefigures above stop matching the disk.